69. FAILURE REQUIREMENT
The integration test must intentionally exercise failures.
Examples:
invalid project
invalid object
invalid revision
stale revision
invalid command
constraint conflict
validation failure
worker failure
document generation failure
duplicate request
Every failure must leave the persistent model consistent.

70. ATOMICITY REQUIREMENT
No operation may leave a partial model mutation.
For example:
MOVE WALL
must not produce:
new wall position
+
missing revision
or:
new revision
+
missing change record
or:
database mutation
+
failed audit event
Required transactional records must succeed together.

71. ASYNCHRONOUS ATOMICITY
Long-running computation requires a different boundary.
The system may create:
JOB = QUEUED
inside a transaction.
The worker then executes the computational operation.
Finalization must verify that the result still corresponds to:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
before committing the derived artifact as current.

72. CURRENT STATE RULE
Only one model version may be designated as the project's current model version.
Only one revision may be designated as the project's current revision.
Historical versions remain available.
Derived outputs may reference historical versions.

73. RECONSTRUCTION REQUIREMENT
Given:
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
the application must be able to reconstruct the associated project state.
The reconstruction process must not depend on the current project state.
This requirement protects historical reproducibility.

74. REPLAY PRINCIPLE
The system should eventually support command replay.
Conceptually:
INITIAL MODEL
 ↓
COMMAND 1
 ↓
COMMAND 2
 ↓
COMMAND 3
 ↓
COMMAND N
 ↓
TARGET MODEL VERSION
The event/change architecture provides the foundation for this capability.
Replay implementation may remain beyond the first executable milestone.

